### How do you implement a RecursiveTask?

```java
package com.bank.parallel;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.RecursiveTask;

public class InterestSumTask extends RecursiveTask<BigDecimal> {

    private static final int THRESHOLD = 10_000;
    private final BigDecimal[] interests;
    private final int from;
    private final int to;

    public InterestSumTask(BigDecimal[] interests, int from, int to) {
        this.interests = interests;
        this.from = from;
        this.to = to;
    }

    @Override
    protected BigDecimal compute() {
        if (to - from <= THRESHOLD) {
            BigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;
            for (int i = from; i < to; i++) {
                sum = sum.add(interests[i]);
            }
            return sum;
        }
        int mid = (from + to) / 2;
        InterestSumTask left = new InterestSumTask(interests, from, mid);
        InterestSumTask right = new InterestSumTask(interests, mid, to);
        left.fork();                         // schedule left asynchronously
        BigDecimal rightResult = right.compute();  // compute right directly
        BigDecimal leftResult = left.join();        // wait for left
        return leftResult.add(rightResult);
    }
}
```

```java
ForkJoinPool pool = ForkJoinPool.commonPool();
BigDecimal total = pool.invoke(new InterestSumTask(interests, 0, interests.length));
System.out.println("Total interest: " + total);
```

### What is the fork-compute-join idiom?

Fork one side, compute the other yourself, then join. This keeps the current thread busy instead of idle.

### How does work stealing work?

Each worker has a double-ended queue. It pushes and pops its own subtasks from one end. When idle, it steals from the other end of a busy worker's queue.

### What is the right threshold?

Too small: millions of tiny tasks, task overhead dwarfs work. Too large: not enough parallelism. A good threshold keeps each leaf task doing a meaningful chunk of CPU work.

### When does fork/join not help?

For small inputs a sequential loop is faster. For blocking I/O the pool threads sit idle. Fork/join is strictly for CPU-bound, splittable problems.

